The browser’s event propagation system is the mechanism by which events travel through the DOM in three phases—capturing, target, and bubbling. It allows both child and parent elements to respond to events, enables event delegation for performance optimization, and provides a consistent and flexible way to manage user interactions. Frameworks like React rely on this system to implement their event handling efficiently.

When an event occurs, the browser processes it in three phases: capturing, target, and bubbling. In the capturing phase, the event travels from the root of the DOM down to the target element, executing capture listeners. In the target phase, the event reaches the actual element where it originated. In the bubbling phase, the event propagates back up the DOM tree, triggering bubble listeners. This structured flow enables flexible and efficient event handling, including delegation and propagation control.

The 3 Phases

    1. Capturing Phase   (top → down)
    2. Target Phase      (actual element)
    3. Bubbling Phase    (bottom → up)

    Why This Design Exists

        This 3-phase model allows:

            1. Early interception (capture)
            2. Exact targeting (target)
            3. Flexible handling (bubble)

Why This System(EVENT PROPOGATION SYSTEM) is Needed

1. Enables Parent Awareness: With propagation, Child clicked → Parent can also react ✅
2. Enables Event Delegation (Performance) : 1 parent → handles all children ✅
his is critical for:

large lists
dynamic UI

3. Supports Flexible Control:
You can:
Stop propagation
e.stopPropagation();

Prevent default behavior
e.preventDefault();

    stopPropagation() prevents the event from propagating further through the DOM tree, stopping parent or ancestor handlers from executing. preventDefault() prevents the browser’s default behavior associated with the event, such as navigation or form submission. They operate independently—one controls event flow, and the other controls browser actions.

    | Feature              | stopPropagation() | preventDefault()    |
    | -------------------- | ----------------- | ------------------- |
    | Purpose              | Stop event flow   | Stop browser action |
    | Affects bubbling     | ✅ Yes             | ❌ No                |
    | Affects capturing    | ✅ Yes             | ❌ No                |
    | Prevents navigation  | ❌ No              | ✅ Yes               |
    | Prevents form submit | ❌ No              | ✅ Yes               |


4. Consistent Event Model

Browser ensures:

predictable order
same behavior across elements

Example
-------

<div id="grandparent">
  <div id="parent">
    <button id="child">Click Me</button>
  </div>
</div>

🔹 Add Event Listeners
----------------------

const grandparent = document.getElementById("grandparent");
const parent = document.getElementById("parent");
const child = document.getElementById("child");

// Capturing phase listeners
grandparent.addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("Grandparent Capture");
}, true);

parent.addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("Parent Capture");
}, true);

child.addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("Child Capture");
}, true);

// Bubbling phase listeners
grandparent.addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("Grandparent Bubble");
});

parent.addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("Parent Bubble");
});

child.addEventListener("click", () => {
  console.log("Child Bubble");
});

