Step-by-Step Internal Flow
==========================
1. User clicks button
2. Browser creates event object
3. Event starts at document

--- CAPTURE ---
4. Travels down DOM tree
5. Executes capture listeners

--- TARGET ---
6. Reaches button
7. Executes target listeners

--- BUBBLE ---
8. Travels upward
9. Executes bubble listeners
10. Ends at document

Important Observations
======================
🔸 1. Default Behavior
Only bubbling phase runs by default
Capture runs only if you specify { capture: true }

In reality, the browser already knows all listeners and just executes them in a fixed order.

How Browser Actually Knows Everything

Every DOM node internally maintains a structure like:

Element
 ├── listeners:
 │     click → [handler1, handler2]
 │     capture click → [handler3]

When you do:

element.addEventListener("click", handler, true);

Browser stores:

eventType: "click"
phase: capture
handler: handler

=========================== Flow
Step 1: Event is Triggered

User clicks button:

<button id="child">

👉 Browser creates event:

{
  type: "click",
  target: childElement
}

👉 target is immediately known

Because:

The browser knows exactly which DOM node received the click (hit-testing).

🔹 🔥 How Browser Finds the Target (Important)

This is NOT magic.

Browser uses layout + coordinates:

Mouse click at (x, y)
   ↓
Browser checks layout tree
   ↓
Finds element at that position
   ↓
That becomes → event.target
🔹 Step 2: Build Propagation Path

Now browser builds a path:

document → html → body → grandparent → parent → child

👉 This is called:

event path / propagation path

🔹 Step 3: Execute in 3 Phases

Now browser runs a loop over this path.

🔹 Phase 1: Capturing (Top → Down)

Browser iterates:

document → html → body → parent → child

At each node:

if (node has capture listeners for "click") {
   execute them
}

👉 No “asking”—just checking stored listeners

🔹 Phase 2: Target Phase

Now browser reaches:

child (target)

👉 It already knows:

event.target === child
🔥 What happens here

Browser executes:

1. Capture listeners on target
2. Bubble listeners on target

👉 That’s why target is a special phase

🔹 Phase 3: Bubbling (Bottom → Up)

Browser iterates backward:

child → parent → grandparent → body → html → document

At each node:

if (node has bubble listeners) {
   execute them
}
🔹 🔥 So What is “Target Phase” Really?

It’s simply the moment when the browser reaches the node that was identified as event.target.

When an event occurs, the browser first determines the target element using hit-testing based on the click coordinates. It then constructs the propagation path from the root to the target. The event is dispatched by iterating over this path—first in the capturing phase, then executing handlers on the target, and finally in the bubbling phase. The browser doesn’t ‘ask’ dynamically; it simply checks pre-registered listeners at each node and executes them in order.

Timeline (Real Internal Flow)
=============================
1. User clicks screen
2. Browser calculates coordinates
3. Finds DOM element → target
4. Builds propagation path
5. Run CAPTURE phase (top → target)
6. Run TARGET phase (at target node)
7. Run BUBBLE phase (target → top)

        Final Mental Model (Accurate)
        =============================
            Click happens
            ↓
            Browser finds exact element (target)
            ↓
            Builds path to root
            ↓
            Iterates path:
            - forward (capture)
            - at target
            - backward (bubble)
            ↓
            Executes matching listeners


